oth the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3\\text{ or }n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge 5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound** – Show that at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny.  \n   *For any column \\(x=a\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) the points \\((a,1),(a,2),\\dots ,(a,n+1-a)\\) lie on distinct vertical lines.  \n   A sunny line can meet a given column in **at most one** point, because a sunny line is not vertical.  \n   Hence covering the whole column needs at least \\(n+1-a\\) distinct lines that are not sunny.  \n   Summation over all columns \\(a\\ge 2\\) forces the existence of at least two non‑sunny lines.  \n   Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).*\n\n2. **Construction for the maximal value \\(k=n-2\\) (all \\(n\\ge5\\)).**  \n   *Take the two non‑sunny lines*  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{v}\\colon x=1 ,\\qquad L_{h}\\colon y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *All points with \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) are covered.  \n   The remaining points form the smaller triangle*\n\n   \\[\n   T_{n-2}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(T_{n-2}\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\) points.  \n   For each integer \\(t\\;(2\\le t\\le n-1)\\) define the sunny line*\n\n   \\[\n   S_{t}\\colon y = (t-2)\\,x - (t-2) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *The line \\(S_{t}\\) passes through the two points*  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{t}= (t,\\,n+2-t),\\qquad Q_{t}= (t+1,\\,n+1-t),\n   \\]\n\n   *both belonging to \\(T_{n-2}\\).  Because the slope of \\(S_{t}\\) equals \\(t-2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny.  \n   The \\(n-2\\) lines \\(\\{S_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\