\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary\n\nFix a column \\(x=a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n\\).  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) on this column are  \n\n\\[\nC_{a}= \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n\\qquad |C_{a}|=n+1-a .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not vertical, therefore it meets the column \\(x=a\\) in **at most one** point.  \nConsequently the points of \\(C_{a}\\) must be covered by at least \\(|C_{a}|\\) distinct lines that are **not** sunny.\n\nSummation of the lower bound (3) over all columns \\(a\\ge 2\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=2}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, (4) forces the existence of at least two non‑sunny lines.  \nHence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines ( \\(n\\ge5\\) )\n\nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}\\colon x=1 ,\\qquad L_{h}\\colon y=1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) and are covered by (6).  \nThe uncovered set is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{Q}_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal{Q}_{n}\\) is exactly the triangle \\(\\mathcal{P}_{\\,n-2}\\) shifted by \\((1,1)\\); it contains  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{Q}_{n}|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{t}\\colon y=(t-2)x-(t-2). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_{t}\\) equals \\(t-2\\); therefore \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny because\n\\(t-2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nThe line \\(S_{t}\\) contains the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_{t}=(t,\\;n+2-t),\\qquad Q_{t}=(t+1,\\;n+1-t),\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nboth of which satisfy \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\); hence they belong to \\(\\mathcal{Q}_{n}\\).\n\nConversely, let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{Q}_{n}\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\ge2\\) we can write \\(b-a=n+2-2a\\).  \nSetting \\(t=a\\) we obtain \\(b = (t-2)a-(t-2)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{t}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal{Q}_{n}\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines\n\\(\\{S_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}\\).\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{n}= \\{L_{v},L_{h}\\}\\cup\\{S_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly two of which are non‑sunny, and it covers all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).  \nHence the value \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines\n\nStarting from the configuration \\(\\mathcal{L}_{n}\\) of the previous section,\nreplace any sunny line \\(S_{t}\\) by one of the already present non‑sunny lines\n\\(L_{v}\\) or \\(L_{h}\\).  \nThe points that were on the removed sunny line are still covered because each of them lies either on the same column (hence on \\(L_{v}\\)) or on the same row (hence on \\(L_{h}\\)).  \n\nEach replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by exactly one while preserving the covering property.  \nPerforming the replacement \\(r\\) times yields a configuration with\n\\(k=n-2-r\\) sunny lines, for any integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n-2\\).  \nThus every integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The cases \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(\\mathcal{P}_{3}\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nA column‑argument identical to that of §1 shows that at least two lines must be non‑sunny; consequently \\(k\\le1\\).  \nThe following two families exhibit the attainable values:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,x=3\\);\n* \\(k=1\\): the sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\) together with the vertica